        "heading": "3.1 ADVERSARIAL ROBUSTNESS FOR CO",
        "text": "Formulation. In general, a CO problem aims to find the optimal solution under a set of constraints (usually encoded by a latent graph). Here we formulate a CO problem Q as:\nmin x c(x, Q) s.t. hi(x, Q) \u2264 0, i = 1, ..., I (1)\nwhere x denotes the decision variable (i.e. solution) that should be discrete, c(x, Q) denotes the cost function given problem instance Q and {hi(x, Q)}Ii=1 represents the set of constraints. For example, in DAG scheduling, the constraints ensure that the solution x, i.e. the execution order of the DAG job nodes, lies in the feasible space and does not conflict the topological dependency structure of Q. However, due to the NP-hard nature, the optimal solution x\u2217 can be intractable within polynomial time. Therefore, we use a solver x\u0302 = f\u03b8(Q), which gives a mapping f\u03b8 : Q \u2192 X to approximate the optimal solution x\u2217. In this work, \u03b8 are solvers\u2019 parameters (e.g. weights and biases in a neural\nsolver, hyperparameters in Gurobi), Q \u2208 Q is the problem instance, and x\u0302 \u2208 X is the approximated solution. x\u0302 also lies in the feasible space that satisfies all the constraints.\nSolvers\u2019 robustness. In this paper, we would like to raise the concern that the estimation capability of the solvers can be unstable and fail on certain hard instances. Thus, we need methods to discover the non-trivial hard instances for the solvers as the robustness metric where a more robust solver can perform better on hard instances. Here non-trivial means these hard instances are not obtained by trivial operations like increasing the size of problems. In short, given a certain solver f\u03b8, we design an attacker (i.e. perturbation model, or hard example miner) g to discover hard instances around existing clean instances Q\u0303 = g(f\u03b8, Q). For example, the attacker can modify the limited number of constraints in Q to get a new problem Q\u0303. Besides working as a robustness metric, the discovered hard instances can guide the parameter setting of solvers, i.e. design solvers\u2019 parameters \u03b8 for better performance (robustness) on hard instances, which is beyond the scope of this paper and we mainly focus on developing the first practically feasible robustness metric. Some preliminary results on hard case utilization are presented in Appendix J.\nRobustness metric. A natural evaluation of the solvers\u2019 robustness is to use the gap c(x\u0302, Q) \u2212 c(x\u2217, Q) (Varma & Yoshida, 2021), where a narrower gap stands for a better performance. However, the optimum x\u2217 is usually unavailable due to the NP-hardness. In this paper, we propose a robustness evaluation method, and the attack is defined by:\nDefinition 1 (Successful Attack). Given a solver f\u03b8 and a clean CO problem instance Q, we obtain a new one Q\u0303 = g(f\u03b8, Q) by the attacker g s.t. the optimal cost value after perturbation will become no-worse, i.e. c(x\u0303\u2217, Q\u0303) \u2264 c(x\u2217, Q). A successful attack occurs when c(f\u03b8(Q\u0303), Q\u0303) > c(f\u03b8(Q), Q).\nThe intuition is that we restrict the attacker to discover a new problem instance with no-worse optimal cost value, such that the gap between the solver and the optimal will be definitely enlarged if the solver gives a worse cost value:\nc(x\u0303\u2217, Q\u0303) \u2264 c(x\u2217, Q), c(f\u03b8(Q\u0303), Q\u0303) > c(f\u03b8(Q), Q)\u21d2 c(f\u03b8(Q), Q)\u2212 c(x\u2217, Q) < c(f\u03b8(Q\u0303), Q\u0303)\u2212 c(x\u0303\u2217, Q\u0303),\n(2)\nwhich represents a successful attack (see Fig. 1(b)).\nAttacker\u2019s target. In conclusion, the attacker should maximize the solver\u2019s cost value after perturbation while conforming to the no-worse restriction. Therefore, we formulate our attack objective:\nmax Q\u0303\nc(f\u03b8(Q\u0303), Q\u0303)\u2212 c(f\u03b8(Q), Q)\ns.t. Q\u0303 = g(f\u03b8, Q), c(x\u0303 \u2217, Q\u0303) \u2264 c(x\u2217, Q)\n(3)\nAttacker\u2019s constraints. Two types of strategies are developed to meet the no worse optimal cost constraint for the attackers: 1) Loosening the constraints of the problem. In this way, the original optimal solution still lies in the feasible space and its cost is not affected, while the enlarged feasible\nspace may include better solutions. Therefore, the new optimal cost cannot be worse than the original one, and we discover this strategy effective for DAG scheduling (see Section 4.1). 2) Modifying a partial problem by lowering the costs. Such a strategy ensures that the cost that corresponds to the original optimal solution can never become worse, and there are also chances to introduce solutions with lower costs. We demonstrate its successful application in traveling salesman (see Section 4.2) and max coverage problems (see Section 4.3 & Appendix B).\nAttack via graph modification. As mentioned above, a CO problem Q can usually be encoded by a latent graph G = (V,E). For example, the DAG problem can be represented by a directed acyclic graph (see Fig. 2), s.t. the graph nodes stand for the DAG jobs, the directed edges denote the sequential constraints for the jobs, and the node features represent the jobs\u2019 completion time. Hence, the perturbation to the problem instance can be conducted by modifications to the graph structure (Dai et al., 2018). Besides, to get non-trivial hard instances around clean examples, the attack is limited within a given attack budget K, e.g. the attacker can remove \u2264 K edges in a DAG problem Q. Typically, in our setting, the graph modification is all conducted by modifying edgesh."
